4.2 Machine Learning Module
FR-ML-001: Spatial Clustering (DBSCAN/HDBSCAN)
Priority: P0 (Critical)
User Story: As a crime analyst, I want to identify crime hotspots with irregular shapes so I can deploy patrols to actual problem areas, not just grid squares.
Description: System shall identify crime hotspots using DBSCAN clustering algorithm to detect non-linear, density-based hotspot boundaries.
Acceptance Criteria:

Implement DBSCAN with Haversine distance metric for lat/lon coordinates
Support configurable parameters:

eps (epsilon): Maximum distance between points (typical range: 0.01-0.1 km)
min_samples: Minimum points to form dense region (typical: 10-30)
Parameters auto-tuned using grid search on initial deployment


Identify hotspot clusters and mark outlier crimes as noise
Generate cluster boundaries as polygon geometries (GeoJSON format)
Store cluster metadata:

cluster_id (integer)
center_point (lat, lon)
radius (km, max distance from center)
crime_count (total crimes in cluster)
primary_crime_types (top 3 crime types by frequency)
risk_score (0-100, calculated separately)


Re-cluster data monthly OR when new data exceeds 5% of training set
Achieve clustering completion within 10 minutes for 100K records
Support HDBSCAN (hierarchical variant) for adaptive density detection

Mathematical Specification:
DBSCAN Parameters:
- eps (ε): 0.5 km (500 meters) default
- min_samples: 15 default
- metric: Haversine (for geographic coordinates)
- algorithm: ball_tree (for efficient spatial queries)

Distance Calculation:
Haversine(lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2) = 2 * R * arcsin(sqrt(
    sin²((lat2-lat1)/2) + cos(lat1)*cos(lat2)*sin²((lon2-lon1)/2)
))
where R = 6371 km (Earth radius)

Output Classifications:
- Core points: Points with ≥ min_samples neighbors within ε
- Border points: Non-core points within ε of a core point
- Noise points: Points not reachable from any core point
- Cluster labels: 0, 1, 2, ... (noise = -1)
Output Format:
json{
  "hotspot_id": "HS-001",
  "cluster_id": 0,
  "boundary": {
    "type": "Polygon",
    "coordinates": [[[lon, lat], [lon, lat], ...]]
  },
  "center_point": {"lat": 40.7128, "lon": -74.0060},
  "radius_km": 0.45,
  "crime_count": 157,
  "primary_crime_types": ["theft", "assault", "harassment"],
  "calculated_at": "2026-01-26T10:00:00Z"
}
Visualization Requirements:

Render cluster boundaries on heatmap as colored polygons
Color intensity based on crime_count or risk_score
Clickable clusters showing detailed statistics

Performance Optimization:

Use spatial indexing (R-tree) for neighbor searches
Parallel processing for large datasets
Incremental clustering for new data (don't re-cluster entire dataset)


FR-ML-002: Temporal Risk Forecasting (LSTM)
Priority: P0 (Critical)
User Story: As a patrol supervisor, I want to predict crime risk for the next 24 hours so I can schedule patrols proactively.
Description: System shall predict hourly crime risk using LSTM neural networks trained on historical time-series crime data.
Acceptance Criteria:

Implement bidirectional LSTM with minimum 2 layers:

Layer 1: 128 hidden units
Layer 2: 64 hidden units


Accept input sequences of 168 hours (7 days) to predict next 24 hours
Include attention mechanism to weight important time periods
Train separate models for:

Each crime category (theft, assault, harassment, vandalism, drug_related)
Overall aggregate risk


Achieve prediction accuracy:

Mean Absolute Error (MAE) < 2.0 for hourly crime count predictions
R² score > 0.70 for correlation between predicted and actual


Update model weights weekly with new crime data
Inference latency < 100ms for single location-time query
Support batch prediction for full city grid (1km x 1km cells)

Model Architecture:
Input Layer: [batch_size, 168 timesteps, n_features]
  ↓
Bidirectional LSTM Layer 1: 128 units
  - return_sequences=True
  - dropout=0.2
  ↓
Attention Mechanism
  - Self-attention weights computed over sequence
  ↓
Bidirectional LSTM Layer 2: 64 units
  - return_sequences=False
  - dropout=0.2
  ↓
Dense Layer: 32 units, ReLU activation
  ↓
Dropout: 0.3
  ↓
Output Layer: 24 units (24-hour forecast), Linear activation

Loss Function: Mean Squared Error (MSE)
Optimizer: Adam (learning_rate=0.001)
Training Data Preparation:

Create sliding windows of 168-hour sequences
Features per timestep:

Hour of day (0-23, one-hot encoded)
Day of week (0-6, one-hot encoded)
Is weekend (binary)
Is holiday (binary)
Weather conditions (temperature, precipitation)
Crime counts (by type)


Normalize features to [0, 1] range
Train/validation/test split: 70/15/15

Training Process:

Batch size: 32
Epochs: 100 (with early stopping, patience=10)
Validation monitoring: MAE on validation set
Save best model based on validation loss
Training time budget: <6 hours on GPU

Inference API:
POST /api/v1/ml/forecast
{
  "location": {"lat": 40.7128, "lon": -74.0060},
  "start_time": "2026-01-26T00:00:00Z",
  "hours_ahead": 24,
  "crime_types": ["theft", "assault"]
}

Response:
{
  "forecasts": [
    {
      "hour": "2026-01-26T00:00:00Z",
      "theft_risk": 0.45,
      "assault_risk": 0.23,
      "overall_risk": 0.38
    },
    ...
  ]
}
Model Monitoring:

Track prediction accuracy over time
Alert if MAE exceeds threshold (>3.0)
Automatic retraining trigger if accuracy degrades >10%


FR-ML-003: Dynamic Contextual Threat Index (DCTI)
Priority: P0 (Critical)
User Story: As a citizen using the app, I want to see a single risk score for my location so I can quickly understand my safety status.
Description: System shall calculate composite risk scores (0-100) for any location-time combination using multi-factor algorithm.
Acceptance Criteria:

Combine multiple risk factors with weighted aggregation:

Historical clustering results: 40% weight
Temporal forecasting: 30% weight
Environmental modifiers: 20% weight
Recent crowdsourced reports: 10% weight


Normalize final score to 0-100 range (integer)
Calculate DCTI in <50ms for single query
Support batch calculation for 10,000+ locations simultaneously
Provide score breakdown showing contribution of each factor (for authority dashboard)

Scoring Formula:
python# Component Scores (each normalized to 0-100)
base_historical_score = get_cluster_risk(lat, lon)  # From DBSCAN
temporal_forecast_score = get_lstm_prediction(lat, lon, time)  # From LSTM
environmental_risk_score = calculate_environmental_risk(time, weather, crowd)
recent_incidents_score = get_recent_reports_score(lat, lon, time_window=7d)

# Weighted Composite
DCTI = (
    base_historical_score * 0.40 +
    temporal_forecast_score * 0.30 +
    environmental_risk_score * 0.20 +
    recent_incidents_score * 0.10
)

# Ensure bounds
DCTI = min(max(DCTI, 0), 100)

# Risk Level Classification
if DCTI >= 81:
    risk_level = "critical"  # Red
elif DCTI >= 61:
    risk_level = "high"  # Orange
elif DCTI >= 31:
    risk_level = "moderate"  # Yellow
else:
    risk_level = "low"  # Green
Component Calculations:
1. Historical Risk (from clustering):
pythondef get_cluster_risk(lat, lon):
    cluster = find_cluster_containing_point(lat, lon)
    if cluster is None:
        return 15  # Base risk for non-hotspot areas
    
    # Risk based on crime density
    density = cluster.crime_count / (π * cluster.radius²)
    density_score = min(density * 10, 60)
    
    # Severity multiplier
    severity_weights = {"low": 1.0, "moderate": 1.5, "high": 2.0, "critical": 2.5}
    avg_severity = calculate_avg_severity(cluster.crimes)
    severity_multiplier = severity_weights[avg_severity]
    
    return min(density_score * severity_multiplier, 100)
2. Temporal Risk (from LSTM):
pythondef get_lstm_prediction(lat, lon, time):
    forecast = lstm_model.predict(lat, lon, time)
    # forecast is crime count prediction (e.g., 0-10 crimes per hour)
    
    # Normalize to 0-100 scale
    # Assume 0 crimes = 0 risk, 5+ crimes = 100 risk
    normalized = min((forecast / 5.0) * 100, 100)
    return normalized
3. Environmental Risk:
pythondef calculate_environmental_risk(time, weather, crowd_density):
    risk = 0
    
    # Time of day
    hour = time.hour
    if 22 <= hour or hour < 6:  # Night (10 PM - 6 AM)
        risk += 15
    
    # Weather visibility
    if weather.visibility < 1000m:  # Low visibility
        risk += 10
    
    # Crowd density (isolation risk)
    if crowd_density < 10:  # Less than 10 people per 100m
        risk += 15
    
    # No street lighting (if data available)
    if lighting_status == "poor":
        risk += 10
    
    return min(risk, 100)
4. Recent Incidents Risk:
pythondef get_recent_reports_score(lat, lon, time_window=7):
    reports = get_crowdsourced_reports(
        lat, lon, 
        radius=500m, 
        since=now() - time_window
    )
    
    if len(reports) == 0:
        return 0
    
    # More recent reports = higher weight
    weighted_sum = 0
    for report in reports:
        days_ago = (now() - report.occurred_at).days
        recency_weight = 1.0 - (days_ago / time_window)
        severity_weight = {"low": 1, "moderate": 2, "high": 3, "critical": 4}[report.severity]
        weighted_sum += recency_weight * severity_weight * 10
    
    return min(weighted_sum, 100)
API Response Format:
json{
  "location": {"lat": 40.7128, "lon": -74.0060},
  "timestamp": "2026-01-26T22:00:00Z",
  "dcti_score": 78,
  "risk_level": "high",
  "risk_factors": {
    "historical": {
      "score": 65,
      "weight": 0.40,
      "contribution": 26.0
    },
    "temporal": {
      "score": 85,
      "weight": 0.30,
      "contribution": 25.5
    },
    "environmental": {
      "score": 40,
      "weight": 0.20,
      "contribution": 8.0
    },
    "recent_incidents": {
      "score": 60,
      "weight": 0.10,
      "contribution": 6.0
    }
  },
  "primary_threats": ["theft", "assault"],
  "recommendations": [
    "Avoid isolated areas",
    "Stay on main streets",
    "Consider alternate route"
  ]
}
Performance Requirements:

Single query: <50ms (p95)
Batch query (1000 locations): <2 seconds
Cache results for 15 minutes (trade-off: freshness vs. load)

Caching Strategy:
Cache Key: "dcti:{lat}:{lon}:{hour}"
TTL: 15 minutes
Invalidation: On new crime data ingestion

FR-ML-004: Model Retraining Pipeline
Priority: P1 (High)
User Story: As a data scientist, I want models to automatically retrain on fresh data so prediction accuracy doesn't degrade over time.
Description: System shall automatically retrain machine learning models on configurable schedules to maintain prediction accuracy.
Acceptance Criteria:

Scheduled retraining:

LSTM: Weekly (every Sunday 2:00 AM)
DBSCAN: Monthly (1st of month, 2:00 AM)


Trigger-based retraining:

When prediction accuracy drops below 75% (based on validation set)
When new crime data exceeds 5% of training set size


Compare new model performance against current production model:

Validation MAE (for LSTM)
Silhouette score (for DBSCAN)


Automatic deployment if new model improves accuracy by >3%
Manual approval required if:

Accuracy degrades
Accuracy improves by <3%
New model architecture introduced


Version all models with:

Timestamp
Performance metrics (MAE, R², silhouette score)
Training data hash
Hyperparameters


Rollback capability to previous model version within 5 minutes
Email notification to administrators on:

Training completion
Deployment
Accuracy degradation alerts



Retraining Workflow:
1. Data Extraction
   - Fetch latest crime data from database
   - Apply same preprocessing as initial training
   
2. Data Validation
   - Check for data drift (distribution changes)
   - Verify minimum record count (10K for LSTM, 5K for DBSCAN)
   
3. Model Training
   - Split data: 70% train, 15% validation, 15% test
   - Train model with same architecture
   - Early stopping on validation loss
   
4. Model Evaluation
   - Calculate performance metrics on test set
   - Compare against current production model
   
5. A/B Testing (Optional for critical updates)
   - Deploy to 10% of traffic
   - Monitor for 24 hours
   - Full rollout if metrics stable
   
6. Deployment Decision
   - If improvement >3%: Auto-deploy
   - If improvement <3%: Queue for manual review
   - If degradation: Alert and halt
   
7. Model Versioning
   - Save to model registry
   - Tag with version number (e.g., lstm_v2.3.1)
   - Store performance report
   
8. Monitoring
   - Track production metrics
   - Compare against baseline
   - Alert if drift detected
Model Registry Schema:
json{
  "model_id": "lstm_v2.3.1",
  "model_type": "lstm",
  "trained_at": "2026-01-26T02:00:00Z",
  "status": "production",
  "metrics": {
    "mae": 1.85,
    "r2_score": 0.73,
    "training_time_minutes": 340
  },
  "data_hash": "abc123def456",
  "hyperparameters": {
    "layers": [128, 64],
    "dropout": 0.2,
    "learning_rate": 0.001
  },
  "deployment_history": [
    {"deployed_at": "2026-01-26T06:00:00Z", "deployed_by": "auto"},
    {"rolled_back_at": null}
  ]
}
Monitoring Dashboard:

Real-time model performance metrics
Training job status
Deployment history
Alert configuration


